--TEST--
A memory breach inside `Core\Arr::from`'s own drain runs the program's `Core\Fatal::onLimit` handler, and the drain never held more than the ceiling on the way to it — `rule:errors/on-limit`, `rule:programs/memory-priority`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:iteration/materialising-a-sequence-core-arr-from
// `rule:errors/on-limit` fires the handler for every resource-limit `FATAL`,
// and this one is raised where no compiled statement is running: the drive is
// native, so the refusal is `crates/nvs-runtime/src/sequence.rs`'s own ask in
// front of a push. The handler runs on the reserve, so it only has something
// to run on if the drain stopped while the reserve was still there.
class Endless {
    // Bounded at four million for the reason the sibling ceiling case gives:
    // sixty-four megabytes of elements is four times the ceiling, so the bound
    // is unreachable in a run this case passes, and a red case fails rather
    // than taking the suite's host with it.
    public static function counter(): Iterator<int> {
        int $i = 0;
        while ($i < 4000000) {
            yield $i;
            $i = $i + 1;
        }
    }
}

Core\Fatal::onLimit(fn (array<string> $report): void => {
    echo "onLimit ", $report['limit'], "\n";
    // The other side of the same bound. The list the drain builds doubles as it
    // grows, and a doubling nobody asked about takes the request past its
    // whole budget before anything reads the balance — which is a handler
    // entered with no reserve left, and one that prints neither line. The mark
    // is recorded as the allocator moves it, so a block taken and given back
    // would still show here.
    if (Core\Budget::memoryPeak() < 16777216) {
        echo "the drain never held more than the ceiling\n";
    } else {
        echo "the drain held more than the ceiling and then reported\n";
    }
});

// `--EXPECT--` is exact, so `kept` not being in it is what asserts the call
// never came back with an array.
echo "before\n";
array<int> $everything = Core\Arr::from(Endless::counter());
echo "kept ", Core\Arr::count($everything), "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The ceiling the drain above is refused at, and nothing else:
# `fatal_reserve_memory` keeps its default so the message names the slice the
# handler runs inside.
[limits]
memory = "16M"
--EXPECT--
before
onLimit memory
the drain never held more than the ceiling
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
FATAL: the request exceeded its memory limit — %d bytes held against a ceiling of 16777216, of which 1048576 is reserved for the limit handler
